<h1>Overhead Conveyor Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">An overhead conveyor system uses an elevated track mounted from the ceiling or structural steel to transport products suspended from trolleys or carriers through production processes. Overhead conveyors keep the floor clear for workers and equipment while moving products through paint lines, assembly stations, ovens, wash systems, and distribution operations. The three main types are <a href="/conveyor-types/monorail-power-free/">monorail and power-and-free</a> (constant speed or independent trolley control), and enclosed track (clean, quiet operation for light loads).</p>
<h2 id="monorail-heading">Monorail Overhead Conveyors</h2>
<p>Monorail overhead conveyors use a single I-beam or enclosed track with a continuous chain that drives all trolleys at the same constant speed. Products hang from the trolleys and move through the process at a uniform pace. The simplicity of monorail systems makes them the most reliable and lowest-maintenance overhead conveyor type.</p>
<h3>I-Beam Track Monorail</h3>
<p>I-beam monorail uses standard or custom I-beam profiles as the track. Trolley wheels ride on the bottom flange of the I-beam. The conveyor chain runs inside or alongside the beam, connecting to trolleys through pusher dogs or attachments. I-beam track handles the heaviest loads (500-2,000 lbs per trolley) and longest spans between supports.</p>
<p>I-beam monorail conveyors serve automotive paint lines, appliance finishing, and heavy manufacturing where large, heavy parts move through wash, paint, and bake processes. Jervis B. Webb (Daifuku), Richards-Wilcox, and PACLINE manufacture I-beam monorail systems. Track profiles range from 3-inch to 8-inch I-beam depending on load and span requirements.</p>
<h3>Enclosed Track Monorail</h3>
<p>Enclosed track monorail uses a tubular or box-section track that encloses the trolley wheels and chain. The enclosed design keeps debris, paint overspray, and contaminants off the trolley wheels and chain, reducing maintenance and preventing contamination of products below. Enclosed track handles lighter loads (10-600 lbs) and suits cleaner environments.</p>
<p>Enclosed track monorail conveyors serve garment handling, light assembly, electronic component transport, and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food processing</a> where contamination from overhead conveyor components is unacceptable. PACLINE, Rapid Industries, and Ultimation offer enclosed track monorail systems.</p>
<h3>Monorail Limitations</h3>
<p>All trolleys move at the same speed and cannot stop independently. If one product needs more time at a process station, all products slow down or the process must adapt to the conveyor speed. Monorail systems cannot accumulate products, every position on the chain moves continuously. For applications requiring variable dwell times, independent stops, or accumulation, power-and-free systems are necessary.</p>
<h2 id="pf-heading">Power-and-Free Systems</h2>
<p>Power-and-free overhead conveyors use a dual-track design. The upper "power" track carries a continuously moving chain. The lower "free" track carries trolleys that can engage and disengage from the power chain independently. When a trolley engages the chain (via a pusher dog mechanism), the chain drives the trolley forward. When the trolley disengages (triggered by a stop, diverter, or accumulation zone), the chain continues while the trolley stays in place.</p>
<h3>Independent Trolley Control</h3>
<p>Each trolley operates independently. One trolley may be moving at chain speed through a paint booth while another trolley stops at an assembly station and a third trolley accumulates in a buffer zone. This independence allows complex production scheduling where different products require different process times at different stations. A product needing 5 minutes of paint cure stops in the oven while a product needing 3 minutes releases earlier.</p>
<h3>Track Switching and Routing</h3>
<p>Power-and-free systems include track switches that route trolleys onto spur lines, bypass loops, and alternate paths. A trolley approaching a switch point receives a routing command (from RFID tag, barcode, or PLC logic) that activates the switch to direct the trolley left, right, or straight. This routing capability creates complex flow networks: a single main loop with multiple process spurs, rework loops, storage banks, and shipping lanes.</p>
<p>Daifuku (Jervis B. Webb), Dematic, and Richards-Wilcox engineer power-and-free systems with dozens of switches and hundreds of trolleys managed by plant-wide control systems. The control software tracks every trolley position, manages routing logic, and optimizes throughput.</p>
<h3>Accumulation Zones</h3>
<p>Power-and-free accumulation zones queue trolleys on the free track while the power chain runs continuously above. Each trolley's pusher dog disengages from the chain as it contacts the trolley ahead of it. Trolleys stack up in the accumulation zone without chain tension or drive force. When the lead trolley releases, the next trolley re-engages the chain and advances. This is conceptually similar to <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">zero-pressure accumulation</a> on floor-level conveyors.</p>
<h3>Elevation Changes</h3>
<p>Power-and-free conveyors handle vertical inclines and declines, carrying products up to mezzanine levels, over equipment, through floor openings, and into elevated ovens or wash tanks. The free track trolleys maintain their load orientation (hanging vertically) through elevation changes up to 45-degree inclines. Vertical drops use controlled-descent track sections.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Overhead Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Paint and finishing lines:</strong> The dominant application for overhead conveyors. Products hang from carriers and travel through multi-stage finishing processes: alkaline wash, rinse, phosphate pretreatment, e-coat, primer spray, topcoat spray, flash-off, and bake cure. Monorail systems serve simple finishing lines with uniform process times. Power-and-free systems serve complex lines where products need different colors, cure times, or process paths.</p>
<p><strong>Powder coating operations:</strong> Overhead conveyors carry metal parts through powder coating booths and cure ovens at 350-450°F. The parts hang vertically for full coverage. Chain and trolley components must withstand oven temperatures. Enclosed track systems keep powder contamination off the conveyor mechanism. Small job shops use monorail; large operations use power-and-free for color change flexibility.</p>
<p><strong>Automotive assembly:</strong> Power-and-free overhead conveyors carry car bodies, doors, hoods, and trim through assembly stations. Workers access the suspended body from all sides without floor conveyors blocking access. The power-and-free design allows each station to hold the body for variable assembly time, critical for mixed-model lines where different vehicle configurations require different work content at each station.</p>
<p><strong>Garment-on-hanger (GOH) systems:</strong> Enclosed track monorail conveyors transport garments on hangers through sorting, quality inspection, pressing, and shipping operations. The garments hang freely, preventing wrinkles. Automated sorting systems read garment RFID tags and route hangers to correct destination lanes. GOH systems serve apparel distribution centers, dry cleaners, and uniform rental operations. Dematic and TGW offer garment sorting conveyor solutions.</p>
<p><strong>Meat and poultry processing:</strong> Overhead monorail conveyors carry carcasses through slaughter, evisceration, inspection, chilling, cutting, and packaging operations. Stainless steel track and trolleys meet <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary requirements</a>. The overhead design keeps product above floor-level contamination sources. Mepaco (now Provisur) and Jarvis Products supply food-grade overhead conveyor systems.</p>
<p><strong>Heavy equipment and aerospace:</strong> Power-and-free conveyors carry large, heavy components (engine assemblies, wing sections, heavy equipment frames) through manufacturing processes. Load capacities up to 4,000 lbs per carrier handle the largest workpieces. The power-and-free design accommodates variable assembly times at each station. Aerospace applications require paint booth, anodizing, and surface treatment transport.</p>
