<h2 id="design-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<p><strong>Structural support loading:</strong> Overhead conveyors transfer product weight plus conveyor weight to the building structure through hangers and support brackets. Calculate point loads at each hanger (product + trolley + chain + track weight) and verify building structural capacity. Existing buildings may require structural reinforcement. New construction should design overhead conveyor loads into the building steel from the start.</p>
<p><strong>Carrier and fixture design:</strong> The carrier (load bar, hook, fixture) connects the product to the trolley. Carrier design affects product accessibility during processing, loading/unloading ergonomics, and product security during transport. Custom carriers for automotive bodies, appliance shells, and aerospace parts represent significant engineering effort. Carriers must withstand paint chemicals, oven temperatures, and mechanical handling forces.</p>
<p><strong>Chain selection and tensioning:</strong> Overhead conveyor chains run under tension from the product load, friction, elevation changes, and curves. Chain pull calculation accounts for total suspended load, coefficient of friction, track inclines, and speed. Chain material must handle the operating temperature: standard carbon steel to 250°F, heat-treated alloy steel to 500°F, stainless steel for corrosive environments. Take-up mechanisms (screw, spring, or gravity) maintain proper chain tension.</p>
<p><strong>Integration with process equipment:</strong> Overhead conveyors must interface with spray booths (product positioning, booth openings, airflow management), ovens (temperature ratings, door openings, dwell time control), wash systems (spray coverage, drainage, chemical resistance), and loading/unloading stations (<a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">vertical lifts</a>, ergonomic work heights, automation interface).</p>
<p><strong>Safety systems:</strong> Overhead conveyors require emergency stops along the entire route, load-drop protection (catch pans, guards), pinch-point guarding at drive and take-up locations, and anti-backup devices on inclines. Power-and-free systems add zone-level stop controls and <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation zone</a> sensing for collision prevention.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is an overhead conveyor system?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>An overhead conveyor uses elevated track to transport suspended products through production processes. Types include monorail (constant speed), power-and-free (independent trolley control), and enclosed track (clean, quiet). See our <a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/">chain conveyor hub</a> for the full chain conveyor family.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Monorail vs power-and-free, which do I need?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Monorail for simple continuous flow at constant speed (single paint color, uniform cure time). Power-and-free for complex routing, variable dwell times, accumulation, and multi-path production. Power-and-free costs substantially more but handles mixed-model production and process variability.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What industries use overhead conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Automotive paint and assembly, powder coating, appliance finishing, garment handling, <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">meat processing</a>, aerospace manufacturing, and heavy equipment assembly. Any operation that benefits from floor-clear transport and overhead process routing.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How much weight can an overhead conveyor carry?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Monorail: 10-2,000 lbs per trolley. Power-and-free: 50-4,000 lbs per carrier. Enclosed track: 10-600 lbs. Load capacity depends on track profile, trolley wheels, chain strength, and structural support. Heavier loads require larger I-beam track and reinforced building structure.</p>

</div>
</div>
